# Exploring the Geometry of Logarithmic Primes

Prime numbers become a bounded constellation under the coordinate $1/\log p$. Begin by moving a point through this nonlinear lens.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The key result is immediate once cardinality meets geometric measure theory: **every countable metric space has zero Hausdorff measure in every positive dimension**. The prime constellation is countable and nonempty, so its Hausdorff dimension is exactly zero.

<details><summary>Click to reveal the covering argument</summary>
Enumerate the points as $x_1,x_2,\ldots$. For any $s>0$, cover $x_n$ by a set whose diameter has $s$-th power at most $\eta 2^{-n}$. The sum of all covering costs is at most $\eta$, and $\eta$ is arbitrary. Nonemptiness supplies nonzero zero-dimensional measure.
</details>

