<details><summary>Why a finite slope is not the Hausdorff dimension</summary>
For a fixed sample of $m$ distinct points, sufficiently small boxes isolate every point, so $N(\varepsilon)=m$. Hence $\log N(\varepsilon)/\log(1/\varepsilon)\to0$. A growing truncation $X(\varepsilon)$ defines a different, coupled statistic whose value depends on the chosen coupling.
</details>

## Twin primes remain geometrically visible

For nearby primes $p<q$, the mean value theorem gives a transformed gap approximately $(q-p)/(p(\log p)^2)$. Twin primes therefore create unusually close pairs, despite having no effect on ordinary Hausdorff dimension.

